                "dsDescriptionValue": {
                  "typeName": "dsDescriptionValue",
                  "multiple": false,
                  "typeClass": "primitive",
                  "value": "<B>Summary of the Study:</B><BR>\nThis study investigated whether <B>role-play in simulated medical consultations</B>, where students alternated between doctor and patient roles, influences <B>intrinsic motivation, relatedness</B>, and <B>competence</B> in learning <B>medical Dutch</B> as a second language (L2). It also explored whether playing the patient role adds educational value.<BR>\n•\t<B>Design</B>: Mixed-methods, one-group pretest–posttest design.<BR>\n•\t<B>Participants</B>: 15 third-year international medical students enrolled in a six-week Medical Dutch course.<BR>\n•\t<B>Procedure</B>:<BR>\no\tEach session: 1.5 hours teaching + 0.5 hours peer role-play (doctor ↔ patient).<BR>\no\tOver the course, each student acted six times as a doctor and six times as a patient.<BR>\no\tInstruments:<BR>\n\t<I>Intrinsic Motivation to Experience Stimulation (IMES)</I> scale.<BR>\n\t<I>Basic Psychological Need Satisfaction</I>scale (relatedness, competence).<BR>\n\tPeer-assessment checklist.<BR>\n\tFinal course grades.<BR>\n\t<I>Semi-structured interviews</I> about patient role experiences .<BR>\no\tAnalysis: Wilcoxon signed-rank test (quantitative) and thematic analysis (qualitative).<BR>\n<B>Key Findings</B>:<BR>\n1.\t<B>Quantitative results</B>:<BR>\no\tIMES increased significantly post-course (Z = –2.35, p = .02).<BR>\no\tRelatedness also increased significantly (Z = –2.11, p = .03).<BR>\no\tCompetence confirmed by self-perception, peer checklist, and grades .<BR>\n2.\t<B>Qualitative themes (5)</B>:<BR>\no\t<I>Motivational experience</I> – role-play created immersion and enjoyment.<BR>\no\t<I>Supportive peer interaction</I> – collaboration and encouragement boosted learning.<BR>\no\t<I>Role-play setup</I> – authenticity of scenarios enhanced realism and language application.<BR>\no\t<I>Learning value of playing the patient</I> – students gained vocabulary, empathy, and communication strategies.<BR>\no\t<I>Novel patient perspective on the doctor’s role</I> – acting as patients increased appreciation for clear, empathetic communication .<BR>\n3.\t<B>Conclusion</B>:<BR>\no\tRole-play enhanced <B>intrinsic motivation, relatedness, and competence</B>, supporting medical L2 learning.<BR>\no\tThe <B>patient role</B>, often overlooked, provided unique added value by offering perspective-taking, empathy, and insight into doctor–patient communication .<BR><BR>"
                },
